  "footnotes": [
    {
      "id": "c-step-1",
      "kind": "correction",
      "html": "True on the first round only. The red-sideband pulse also turns every lower pair $|g\\rangle|n\\rangle \\leftrightarrow |e\\rangle|n-1\\rangle$ at its own $\\eta\\kOmega\\sqrt{n}$, and the bare-resonance pulse of step (2) mixes $|g\\rangle|n\\rangle$ with $|e\\rangle|n\\rangle$ on every rung, so on later rounds $|e\\rangle|N-1\\rangle$ already holds amplitude and a $\\pi$ pulse would swap it back into $|g\\rangle|N\\rangle$. The pulse that empties $|g\\rangle|N\\rangle$ has the area and phase that turn the pair’s two amplitudes wholly onto $|e\\rangle|N-1\\rangle$, worked out from the whole state at that round (C. K. Law and J. H. Eberly, Phys. Rev. Lett. 76, 1055 (1996)); it is never longer than a $\\pi$ pulse."
    },
    {
      "id": "c-step-2",
      "kind": "correction",
      "html": "The length alone is not enough: $|g\\rangle|N-1\\rangle$ already holds amplitude, and the bare-resonance pulse empties $|e\\rangle|N-1\\rangle$ into it only when the laser’s phase $\\kphi$ is chosen too, so that the two amplitudes add on $|g\\rangle|N-1\\rangle$."
    }
  ]
